//! task: write
//! ask: Write a higher-order function `applica` that applies a function argument to a value and requires exactly the capabilities its argument requires.
//! expect: ok
//! refs: §4.2
//! explain: A capability row is part of a function type. `poscit sicut f` means "the row carried by the type of parameter `f`", substituted positionally at each call site. A caller passing a pure function pays nothing; one passing a function that needs `rete` must declare `rete` itself, which is what makes laundering impossible rather than merely annotated. `[OPEN]`: the call through `f` type-checks and cannot be emitted yet, because indirect calls are a refused opcode (§9.2); measured, `exsc -o` on it terminates with `SIGILL`, so this is checked and not run.

publica functio applica(v: u32, f: functio(u32) -> u32) -> u32 poscit sicut f {
    redde f(v);
}

functio duplica(x: u32) -> u32 {
    redde x * 2;
}

publica functio usus() -> u32 {
    redde applica(21, duplica);
}
